WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:16.090
And the nice thing
about learning bslib

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:19.240
is it serves as a
general foundation

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:23.410
that not only Shiny and R
Markdown directly can use,

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:27.070
but also extension packages
like flexdashboard,

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:32.020
and pkgdown and bookdown, like
some of these other packages

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:34.630
that fit into the
ecosystem and essentially,

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:40.730
like extend R Markdown
or extend Shiny

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:45.960
because of how we've sort
of approached this problem.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.530
So at least, a handful
of these packages

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:53.720
have pretty nice seamless
integration with the ability

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.030
to like if you have a
theme that you're already

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:58.430
using for your
Shiny application,

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:01.070
you can pretty
easily sort of copy

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:03.350
paste that over to
a flexdashboard app

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:07.310
or your pkgdown website
or something like that.

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:08.960
If you're using
bslib, make sure you

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.740
have a more recent version
of Shiny and R Markdown

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.770
when you're using it.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:18.680
And the real
straightforward basic usage

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:23.720
is to take advantage
of this theme argument.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:26.420
If we're talking
about Shiny, there's

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:31.620
a few different ways to create
sort of the page container.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.910
So if you want that
classic navbar page look,

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:37.520
you would start
with navbar page,

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:42.830
and give the theme argument
to this bs_theme object.

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:46.610
And with this
bs_theme function, you

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:51.120
can specify the bootstrap
version that you want to use.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.460
So I think still we're
defaulting to bootstrap four,

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:58.760
but soon we will default
to bootstrap five.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:03.450
But if you want your project
to be fully reproducible,

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:07.070
we do recommend like fully
specify the version so

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:09.770
that if we change the
default in the future,

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:12.420
you won't be surprised that
you're getting a new bootstrap

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:12.920
version.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:17.900
So this is kind of
like the easiest

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:21.890
way to opt in to a
new bootstrap version

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:26.210
is to call this bs_theme
function, give it a version,

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:31.580
and pass it to the theme
argument of a page layout.

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:34.220
And the equivalent
way of doing something

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:37.400
like this in R Markdown
is to work with the theme

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:41.630
arguments of a output format.

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:46.670
So not all output formats
will have a theme arguments.

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:51.920
This historically has just been
used for output formats that

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:54.980
are HTML-based and
theme has historically

00:02:54.980 --> 00:03:01.155
meant like choose a different
bootswatch theme essentially.

00:03:01.155 --> 00:03:04.470
So you might have used this
and said something like theme

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:11.190
Darkly or theme Cyborg
or some Bootswatch theme,

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:14.070
but we've now extended this
interface, essentially,

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:17.580
with bslib so that you
can provide any collection

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:20.340
of arguments to this
bs_theme() function with all

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:22.860
of your custom
theming parameters.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:27.930
And then essentially,
this HTML document

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:31.380
will use all of
those custom settings

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:35.250
to influence the CSS
that goes on the page

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:38.130
in the same way that would
happen for the navbar page.

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:43.260
So that's one of the great
things about learning

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:47.780
via bslib once, is you could use
it in both of those projects.

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:49.790
You just have to kind
of work around the fact

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:54.050
that this is YAML,
and not R code.

00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:57.470
So some of the things that
you might be more easily done

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:02.210
over here in the R side might
be a little bit trickier

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:04.820
to convert that to pure YAML.

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:10.280
If you have more recent versions
of bslib and flexdashboard,

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:15.350
bslib itself comes with some
different templates in RStudio.

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:19.820
So I could show
you how that looks.

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:27.450
If I go to File,
New file in RStudio

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:32.010
and then go to R Markdown,
this little window will pop up.

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:36.580
And then if you choose
the From Template option,

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:42.180
bslib will provide a few
different examples of how

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:45.958
to get started with R Markdown.

00:04:45.958 --> 00:04:48.000
And I believe-- I don't
know if I have the newest

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:50.910
version of flexdashboard,
but we also

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:55.230
have some flexdashboard
templates that show you

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:58.720
how to use bslib with it.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:08.460
So if I just open that one, this
will give me a more worked-out,

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:11.790
complete theme.

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:15.360
And so it's not going to be
super clear or super obvious.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:20.340
Like, one unfortunate way
about how this currently works,

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:22.980
which we're looking to
improve in the future,

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:29.960
is that, how am I going
to remember what arguments

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:33.800
go to this theme parameter?

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:36.980
I'm using bg here, which
represents the background

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:40.610
color, fg which
represents the foreground

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:44.150
color, a primary
color, secondary,

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:47.990
and a couple different
font settings.

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:55.060
And I guess the way that
I keep track of this--

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:58.510
and I'm hoping other people
will remember this as well--

00:05:58.510 --> 00:06:06.490
is you can pass in any argument
to this bs_theme() function

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:10.670
to this theme YAML
setting over here.

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:15.550
So as you can see, the first few
listed are the bg, as I said,

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:17.860
a color string for
the background;

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:21.130
fg, color string
for the foreground.

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:26.380
There's a handful of different
accent color settings.

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:30.010
So primary is going to be
the most obvious one that

00:06:30.010 --> 00:06:32.120
changes the most colors.

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:36.700
But then it's also possible
to control some other colors

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:40.180
with these other
accent color settings.

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:44.200
There's three different
main settings for fonts.

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:47.950
So the base font is going to
be kind of like the global font

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:50.560
setting for everything
on the page.

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:56.560
Code will target code
blocks, essentially,

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:59.260
headings for things like
titles, and then you

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:02.920
can also do some
things like scale

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:06.780
the fonts to larger sizes.

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:09.225
And eventually,
we'll get through--

00:07:13.090 --> 00:07:17.340
talk about the fact that there's
actually hundreds of different

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:23.490
theming controls that you
have access to via bs_theme().

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:25.680
But they're not all
named arguments,

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:28.620
and we've kind of intentionally
designed things that way,

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:33.730
where we want to
allow you to set

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:37.150
any of these main
color and font settings

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:40.390
and have it work across
the board on all Bootstrap

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:42.820
versions.

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:44.920
But then when you
get more targeted

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:51.100
and set a more specific style
on a very particular thing

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:57.490
on a website, we want to
make it a little bit harder

00:07:57.490 --> 00:08:02.440
to discover those things and
make you aware of the fact

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:05.650
that this could potentially
break with a newer

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:07.420
version of Bootstrap,
and we're not

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:09.580
committing to supporting
this across the board.

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:15.660
So let me actually just
show you a real example

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:19.310
of what's going on here.

00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:24.020
So let me just knit this.

00:08:24.020 --> 00:08:28.020
All right, so this
is the template,

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:31.440
or the result of that template.

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:35.789
So that main bg setting is
going in and determining

00:08:35.789 --> 00:08:37.950
this background color here.

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:42.929
The foreground color,
I believe, will be used

00:08:42.929 --> 00:08:47.350
for these text colors here.

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:51.390
And the thing about implementing
a dark theme like this

00:08:51.390 --> 00:08:53.190
that you might want
to keep in mind

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:59.040
is there's actually at
least tens, if not hundreds,

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:03.150
of actual color styles
that are going into the end

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:05.650
result on the page.

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:10.200
So for example, the gray on
this background for this code

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:14.400
block is actually like a
mixture between the background

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:18.670
and the foreground color
that you're choosing.

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:21.720
So when you give us
those two main settings,

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:25.470
you want it to be kind
of a high contrast

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:32.590
difference between probably
two grayscale colors.

00:09:32.590 --> 00:09:35.970
So that's why I recommend--

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:40.920
first of all, it's
primarily most

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:43.950
useful for just implementing
a dark theme like this, where

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:46.290
you just set the background
to something that's

00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:48.120
close to black and
foreground that's

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:50.340
something close to white.

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:53.910
Then you can easily
have a dark theme here.

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:58.050
But I think people
quickly want to set colors

00:09:58.050 --> 00:10:01.560
with different hues, like
a green for a background

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:02.580
or something like that.

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:06.600
And just be careful if you want
to do something like that--

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:08.940
you want to set a dark green--

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:12.810
choose a lighter green
for the foreground color,

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:15.630
and don't choose a light
red or something like that,

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:20.160
because you'll get mixtures
of those two colors

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:22.950
to determine stuff
like these code blocks.

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:34.260
Also, I've kind of already
jumped one of the punchlines

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:39.350
here, where this template
is also showing you

00:10:39.350 --> 00:10:42.050
another package that
could be kind of thought

00:10:42.050 --> 00:10:45.604
of as like a sister
package to bslib

00:10:45.604 --> 00:10:50.150
called thematic, which
essentially allows you

00:10:50.150 --> 00:10:58.470
to pretty easily
translate on some level

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:01.710
the CSS styles that
are going onto the page

00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:06.810
and have those styles actually
determine new global styling

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:11.730
defaults for your R plots.

00:11:11.730 --> 00:11:16.710
So to make that happen, I
just called thematic_rmd().

00:11:16.710 --> 00:11:19.170
Since this is an R
Markdown document,

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:26.580
thematic has a thematic_rmd(),
where this extra setting will

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:32.760
also make it so that it uses the
font settings that I've set up

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:38.550
here in this document as well to
use that for the fonts that are

00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:40.950
in the plot.

00:11:40.950 --> 00:11:44.190
And the reason why this isn't
really enabled by default

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:50.850
is that chances are
you don't actually

00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:55.740
have the font installed
in your R graphics device.

00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:59.760
So thematic will actually--

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:01.920
in the case that it's
detected that you don't

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:04.080
have that font installed,
it will actually

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:13.040
go out and grab fonts
from Google's font API.

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:14.912
So if you're not
familiar, there's

00:12:14.912 --> 00:12:19.900
a fonts.google.com
that provides you

00:12:19.900 --> 00:12:22.030
access to hundreds,
if not thousands,

00:12:22.030 --> 00:12:24.700
of different free fonts.

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:31.570
And essentially, we've tried to
design both bslib and thematic

00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:34.210
in the sense of, if
you can find the font

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:40.120
on this fonts.google.com,
you don't

00:12:40.120 --> 00:12:43.900
have to do any other work
beyond just specifying the font,

00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:47.050
and we'll do all the work
of downloading and caching

00:12:47.050 --> 00:12:50.320
and making sure that that
font is actually available

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:56.000
when people try to view your
website at the end of the day.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:58.630
So there's a lot of work to
make that all that magic happen

00:12:58.630 --> 00:13:01.255
in the background, if you've
ever worked with fonts before.

00:13:04.420 --> 00:13:07.260
And I kind of
glossed over the fact

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:09.690
that there is kind
of a special way

00:13:09.690 --> 00:13:13.710
to use Google Fonts with bslib.

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:22.110
If you're inside of R Markdown,
you first use this bs_theme()

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:25.320
function and remind
yourself, oh, yeah,

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:27.630
I want to change the base font.

00:13:27.630 --> 00:13:34.380
And if you want to use this with
the normal R programming usage,

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:38.130
you have to remember, oh,
I want to actually use this

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:43.380
font_google() helper and
then give the family name.

00:13:43.380 --> 00:13:45.910
And that's just kind of an
indication to tell bslib,

00:13:45.910 --> 00:13:49.680
hey, this is actually
a font that I

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:53.940
want you to make sure the font
files are actually included

00:13:53.940 --> 00:13:59.760
with the end website and
are actually made available

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:04.030
and used for that
based on setting.

00:14:04.030 --> 00:14:05.940
Because if you were
to do it this way,

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:10.980
it would just change the CSS
style without actually bringing

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:15.855
in any of the actual font files
needed to render that font.

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:23.910
So yeah, that's an important
distinction to remember,

00:14:23.910 --> 00:14:28.110
that this is the way you would
want to specify something

00:14:28.110 --> 00:14:32.640
like this on the R
side, like with Shiny.

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:36.900
But this is the way you
specify it in R Markdown.

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:42.090
Let me just go back to a more
simple example with Shiny.

00:14:42.090 --> 00:14:46.230
So this is kind of like the
example we saw on the home page

00:14:46.230 --> 00:14:46.770
for bslib.

00:14:50.340 --> 00:14:55.590
And let's just
create a navbar page

00:14:55.590 --> 00:14:59.830
with the typical
navbar page usage.

00:14:59.830 --> 00:15:03.270
So this is just a
simple Shiny app,

00:15:03.270 --> 00:15:06.180
where I have two
tabs here, A and B,

00:15:06.180 --> 00:15:12.590
and I can click back and forth
to look at page A and page B.

00:15:12.590 --> 00:15:14.220
And that's fine.

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:18.230
That works just fine.

00:15:18.230 --> 00:15:23.960
But let me actually show
you how I would actually

00:15:23.960 --> 00:15:27.950
take this theme from
my R Markdown document

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:31.730
and bring that
into my Shiny app.

00:15:31.730 --> 00:15:35.540
So I will take those arguments--

00:15:35.540 --> 00:15:41.120
oop, try to get
multiple cursors here--

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:44.980
change these colons
to equal, make

00:15:44.980 --> 00:15:48.400
sure I have commas at the end.

00:15:48.400 --> 00:15:55.100
Base_font, remember, requires a
special font_google() function.

00:15:59.830 --> 00:16:03.250
And do that for the base_front
and the heading_font.

00:16:11.780 --> 00:16:15.140
And now I should be able to
run that and have the same sort

00:16:15.140 --> 00:16:17.510
of theme look to--

00:16:17.510 --> 00:16:19.880
whoop, something
went wrong here.

00:16:19.880 --> 00:16:23.030
Forgot a comma-- wonder
how much my life has

00:16:23.030 --> 00:16:24.260
been wasted on that problem.

00:16:27.210 --> 00:16:30.110
So yeah, now we got the new
different fonts in there.

00:16:30.110 --> 00:16:34.250
And again, like the
navbar background color

00:16:34.250 --> 00:16:39.860
is another mixture between
this darkest black background

00:16:39.860 --> 00:16:43.940
color that is going to be used
for the body and the foreground

00:16:43.940 --> 00:16:45.545
color that's used for the text.

00:16:51.920 --> 00:16:55.640
And these are just some
warnings that I actually don't

00:16:55.640 --> 00:16:59.510
have the best contrast ratio.

00:16:59.510 --> 00:17:01.610
And this is something that--

00:17:01.610 --> 00:17:05.990
this is kind of like a side
thing that I think more

00:17:05.990 --> 00:17:08.599
advanced Shiny developers
might want to know about,

00:17:08.599 --> 00:17:12.380
that more recently, we've added
a function in Shiny called

00:17:12.380 --> 00:17:18.609
devmode(), where we kind of set
at least a handful of different

00:17:18.609 --> 00:17:22.599
options for you that are
convenient for developing Shiny

00:17:22.599 --> 00:17:24.770
applications.

00:17:24.770 --> 00:17:27.910
So I actually have this
set in my R profile,

00:17:27.910 --> 00:17:30.280
and this is a warning
that I will only

00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:35.650
get in Development mode that,
obviously, we don't always

00:17:35.650 --> 00:17:38.410
want to be throwing these
warnings when you're running

00:17:38.410 --> 00:17:41.030
this Shiny app in production.

00:17:41.030 --> 00:17:43.090
So it's something we just like--

00:17:43.090 --> 00:17:46.000
if we have warnings or other
things that we think only

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:53.710
developers should see, that's
something we'll only emit when

00:17:53.710 --> 00:17:56.770
you have this devmode() set.

00:17:56.770 --> 00:17:59.770
And to set it, all you need
to do is call that function.

00:18:03.280 --> 00:18:06.220
All right, but let's do
a little bit more here.

00:18:06.220 --> 00:18:09.170
As I mentioned,
this bs_theme()--

00:18:11.820 --> 00:18:15.990
we really only have, like,
10 or 15 different arguments

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:17.940
documented here--

00:18:17.940 --> 00:18:20.370
the Bootstrap version,
a Bootswatch theme

00:18:20.370 --> 00:18:23.490
if you want one,
and then the more

00:18:23.490 --> 00:18:27.630
main color and font settings.

00:18:27.630 --> 00:18:32.600
But if you want to get more
targeted with your theming,

00:18:32.600 --> 00:18:37.170
hopefully we have a link here--

00:18:37.170 --> 00:18:38.240
and not a great link.

00:18:38.240 --> 00:18:40.310
We could probably provide
a better link here

00:18:40.310 --> 00:18:42.650
to this article.

00:18:42.650 --> 00:18:45.470
On the bslib
website, you'll find

00:18:45.470 --> 00:18:49.190
this dropdown has
both Bootstrap 5

00:18:49.190 --> 00:18:52.700
and Bootstrap 4 Sass variables.

00:18:52.700 --> 00:18:55.460
And this is a big
interactive table

00:18:55.460 --> 00:18:59.060
with hundreds of
different theme settings

00:18:59.060 --> 00:19:03.250
for customizing lots
of different things

00:19:03.250 --> 00:19:05.920
that go into Bootstrap.

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:08.920
Just know, if you're
new to Bootstrap,

00:19:08.920 --> 00:19:11.410
there's a lot more here
than you probably even want

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:18.470
to know about because there
are certain components

00:19:18.470 --> 00:19:24.170
that Bootstrap makes available
that we, at least currently,

00:19:24.170 --> 00:19:30.640
don't make that super
available to you in Shiny.

00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:34.110
So there are things like--

00:19:34.110 --> 00:19:37.050
just give you a couple of
examples-- like, in Bootstrap

00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:39.630
itself, there are
some components

00:19:39.630 --> 00:19:50.010
like accordions and alert
boxes and carousels,

00:19:50.010 --> 00:19:51.660
some different
components that we

00:19:51.660 --> 00:19:55.350
don't provide official R
wrappers for in the Shiny

00:19:55.350 --> 00:19:56.010
package.

00:19:56.010 --> 00:20:00.480
But some extension packages
make this stuff available.

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:03.300
There's a bsplus
package that has

00:20:03.300 --> 00:20:08.070
wrapped some of these components
and some other projects

00:20:08.070 --> 00:20:09.610
out there.

00:20:09.610 --> 00:20:12.930
But you can also use
those things more directly

00:20:12.930 --> 00:20:20.820
by essentially taking this
HTML to implement a carousel

00:20:20.820 --> 00:20:22.560
and writing that in R yourself.

00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:28.850
So if you actually wanted
to do something like that--

00:20:28.850 --> 00:20:30.095
just to quickly show you--

00:20:35.840 --> 00:20:38.990
you could basically copy
that HTML over and say,

00:20:38.990 --> 00:20:44.790
I want this div with an ID
of this in a class of this

00:20:44.790 --> 00:20:47.710
and a data-bs-ride of that.

00:20:51.280 --> 00:20:55.530
So there is an actual R API
that Shiny provides to you

00:20:55.530 --> 00:20:58.620
to write that same
HTML as R code

00:20:58.620 --> 00:21:00.510
and include that
in your Shiny app.

00:21:00.510 --> 00:21:03.480
And lots of people do that--

00:21:03.480 --> 00:21:07.560
like write custom HTML.

00:21:07.560 --> 00:21:12.390
So it would be possible to
just write the same HTML

00:21:12.390 --> 00:21:15.480
and get a carousel, but I'm
not going to actually go

00:21:15.480 --> 00:21:19.110
through a real example of that.

00:21:19.110 --> 00:21:23.130
But just to kind of point out,
there's a whole lot of stuff

00:21:23.130 --> 00:21:26.380
that Bootstrap exposes to you.

00:21:26.380 --> 00:21:30.240
And that's why there's hundreds
of different Sass variables,

00:21:30.240 --> 00:21:38.280
is because if you
search this table here

00:21:38.280 --> 00:21:40.695
for something like
an accordion, there

00:21:40.695 --> 00:21:44.550
are settings for setting the
padding on the accordion,

00:21:44.550 --> 00:21:47.010
the background color,
the border width,

00:21:47.010 --> 00:21:50.310
the border-- like, pretty much
every single, little, small

00:21:50.310 --> 00:21:55.110
detail that would make sense
to have a high-level control

00:21:55.110 --> 00:22:01.540
for, they'll provide it to
you through this Sass variable

00:22:01.540 --> 00:22:02.760
theming interface.

00:22:06.051 --> 00:22:08.490
And not only that,
we've actually

00:22:08.490 --> 00:22:12.120
done some work to make some
common things a bit easier

00:22:12.120 --> 00:22:15.100
for Shiny app developers.

00:22:15.100 --> 00:22:20.400
So one thing in particular is
this navbar background color.

00:22:20.400 --> 00:22:23.190
It's not an actual
Bootstrap thing,

00:22:23.190 --> 00:22:26.640
but we just added it to
make your life easier,

00:22:26.640 --> 00:22:34.200
where if you wanted to take
this basic navbar page,

00:22:34.200 --> 00:22:38.970
and instead of-- let me
just refresh our memory here

00:22:38.970 --> 00:22:40.890
and point out--

00:22:40.890 --> 00:22:47.160
this kind of 50% gray
essentially being used

00:22:47.160 --> 00:22:49.590
for the background
of this navbar,

00:22:49.590 --> 00:22:59.040
I could set this
"navbar-bg" to "purple."

00:22:59.040 --> 00:23:04.580
Now I have a purple
background for my navbar.

00:23:04.580 --> 00:23:08.780
And not only that, but bslib
will do some extra work

00:23:08.780 --> 00:23:14.250
to make sure that you get a
sensible contrast by default.

00:23:14.250 --> 00:23:20.660
So if you wanted a
light blue instead,

00:23:20.660 --> 00:23:25.430
notice how on this
more darker purple,

00:23:25.430 --> 00:23:30.770
you get a light foreground color
to contrast that dark color.

00:23:30.770 --> 00:23:34.760
But now if I do a light
color on the navbar,

00:23:34.760 --> 00:23:39.080
it's flipped the text color
to dark instead of light.

00:23:41.780 --> 00:23:44.230
[STRINGS STRUMMING]

